or=\"top\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\"> Montr&eacute;al</text><line x1=\"113.3984603881836\" y1=\"423.84000000000003\" x2=\"140.3984603881836\" y2=\"423.84000000000003\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"1.1 6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" marker-end=\"url(#marker2)\"/><line x1=\"140.3984603881836\" y1=\"423.84000000000003\" x2=\"167.3984603881836\" y2=\"423.84000000000003\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"1.1 6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" marker-start=\"url(#marker2)\"/><text transform=\"translate(177.3984603881836 430.84000000000003)\" text-anchor=\"top\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\"> Qu&eacute;bec City</text><line x1=\"113.3984603881836\" y1=\"455.84000000000003\" x2=\"140.3984603881836\" y2=\"455.84000000000003\" stroke=\"#999\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" marker-end=\"url(#marker3)\"/><line x1=\"140.3984603881836\" y1=\"455.84000000000003\" x2=\"167.3984603881836\" y2=\"455.84000000000003\" stroke=\"#999\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" marker-start=\"url(#marker3)\"/><text transform=\"translate(177.3984603881836 462.84000000000003)\" text-anchor=\"top\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\"> Toronto</text><line x1=\"124.16718816757202\" y1=\"252\" x2=\"192.97656297683716\" y2=\"248\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" marker-end=\"url(#marker0)\" marker-start=\"url(#marker0)\"/><line x1=\"124.16718816757202\" y1=\"200.66666666666666\" x2=\"192.97656297683716\" y2=\"178\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-dasharray=\"9 6\" marker-end=\"url(#marker1)\" marker-start=\"url(#marker1)\"/><line x1=\"124.16718816757202\" y1=\"232\" x2=\"192.97656297683716\" y2=\"230.66666666666666\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"1.1 6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" marker-end=\"url(#marker2)\" marker-start=\"url(#marker2)\"/><line x1=\"124.16718816757202\" y1=\"234.66666666666666\" x2=\"192.97656297683716\" y2=\"214.66666666666666\" stroke=\"#999\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" marker-end=\"url(#marker3)\" marker-start=\"url(#marker3)\"/><line x1=\"192.97656297683716\" y1=\"248\" x2=\"261.7859377861023\" y2=\"246\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" marker-end=\"url(#marker0)\" marker-start=\"url(#marker0)\"/><line x1=\"192.97656297683716\" y1=\"178\" x2=\"261.7859377861023\" y2=\"127.33333333333331\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-dasharray=\"9 6\" marker-end=\"url(#marker1)\" marker-start=\"url(#marker1)\"/><line x1=\"192.97656297683716\" y1=\"230.66666666666666\" x2=\"261.7859377861023\" y2=\"230\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"1.1 6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" marker-end=\"url(#marker2)\" marker-start=\"url(#marker2)\"/><line x1=\"192.97656297683716\" y1=\"214.66666666666666\" x2=\"261.7859377861023\" y2=\"151.33333333333331\" stroke=\"#999\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" marker-end=\"url(#marker3)\" marker-start=\"url(#marker3)\"/><line x1=\"261.7859377861023\" y1=\"246\" x2=\"330.59531259536743\" y2=\"244.66666666666666\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" marker-end=\"url(#marker0)\" marker-start=\"url(#marker0)\"/><line x1=\"261.7859377861023\" y1=\"127.33333333333331\" x2=\"330.59531259536743\" y2=\"93.33333333333334\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-dasharray=\"9 6\" marker-end=\"url(#marker1)\" marker-start=\"url(#marker1)\"/><line x1=\"261.7859377861023\" y1=\"230\" x2=\"330.59531259536743\" y2=\"226\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"1.1 6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" marker-end=\"url(#marker2)\" marker-start=\"url(#marker2)\"/><line x1=\"261.7859377861023\" y1=\"151.33333333333331\" x2=\"330.59531259536743\" y2=\"133.33333333333331\" stroke=\"#999\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" marker-end=\"url(#marker3)\" marker-start=\"url(#marker3)\"/></g></svg></figure><div class=\"sr-only\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Long description for line graph titled Census Data for Four Canadian Cities, 1871–1901\"><ul>\n<li>The following 4 lines are shown:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Halifax</li>\n<li>Montr&eacute;al</li>\n<li>Qu&eacute;bec City</li>\n<li>Toronto</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The Halifax line:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Begins at 1871, 30 thousand</li>\n<li>Rises gradually to 1881, 36 thousand&nbsp;</li>\n<li>Rises gradually to 1891, 39 thousand</li>\n<li>Rises gradually to 1901, 41 thousand</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The Montr&eacute;al line:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Begins at 1871, 107 thousand</li>\n<li>Rises gradually to 1881, 141 thousand&nbsp;</li>\n<li>Rises sharply to 1891, 217 thousand</li>\n<li>Rises sharply to 1901, 268 thousand</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The Qu&eacute;bec City line:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Begins at 1871, 60 thousand</li>\n<li>Rises gradually to 1881, 62 thousand&nbsp;</li>\n<li>Rises gradually to 1891, 63 thousand</li>\n<li>Rises gradually to 1901, 69 thousand</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The Toronto line:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Begins at 1871, 56 thousand</li>\n<li>Rises gradually to 1881, 86 thousand&nbsp;</li>\n<li>Rises sharply to 1891, 181 thousand</li>\n<li>Rises gradually to 1901, 208 thousand</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p>The first national census in Canada was conducted in <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1871</span>. The graph shows the population sizes of some of the largest Canadian cities from each of the first four national censuses, which occurred every ten years.</p>","stem":"<p>According to the graph, which city had the largest population in <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1891</span>?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"09cf7f70-9cab-45c7-b942-fda828417176","content":"<p>Toronto</p>"},{"id":"9e994dd2-f3cd-4417-a747-5e6c1b73cd59","content":"<p>Montr&eacute;al</p>"},{"id":"5b9de4f6-5998-420e-bd1d-03a99a23977c","content":"<p>Qu&eacute;bec City</p>"},{"id":"231e5659-93ab-4ee8-81e4-603c91235aa9","content":"<p>Halifax</p>"}],"keys":["9e994dd2-f3cd-4417-a747-5e6c1b73cd59"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because the graph shows that Montr&eacute;al had the largest population in 1891 among the four cities represented. According to the data points in the graph for the year 1891, Montr&eacute;al&rsquo;s population was approximately 220,000, which is greater than the respective populations of Toronto (approximately 180,000), Qu&eacute;bec City (approximately 60,000), and Halifax (approximately 40,000).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because Toronto&rsquo;s population in 1891 was approximately 180,000, which is the second largest among the four cities shown but still clearly smaller than Montr&eacute;al&rsquo;s population of approximately 220,000. Choice C is incorrect because Qu&eacute;bec City had a population of approximately 60,000 in 1891, making it only the third largest of the four cities shown. Choice D is incorrect because Halifax had a population of approximately 40,000 people in 1891. Of the four cities shown in the graph, Halifax has the smallest population, not the largest.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771170,"updateDate":1776362771170}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771170, 1776362771170),
    ($SATQ$23a7038f$SATQ$, $SATQ$5a912020-242d-4bc0-96dd-c00c12736562$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$4658a564-1792-494d-9fdf-a8f541d02efd$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Shimmering is a collective defense behavior that researchers have observed in giant honeybee colonies. When shimmering, different groups of bees flip their bodies up and down in what looks like waves. This defense is initiated when hornets hover near a colony, serving to deter the hornets from approaching the bees. Researchers hypothesize that this behavior is a specialized defense response to hornets, as it is not observed when other, larger predators approach the colony.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"7238571c-4a6b-4cdd-b2d2-7af73c726aaf","content":"<p>Researchers are unsure how giant honeybees defend against predators larger than hornets.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"a6a2bdf8-c464-409e-b5ec-a73aa3c4cbdc","content":"<p>Researchers think that shimmering in giant honeybees is a specific defense against hornets.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"09ebed01-efe3-4221-ad1b-d60645f5cf85","content":"<p>Hornets are known to be the main predator of giant honeybees.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"cac1bb64-8101-4f4a-8346-6beae5ae78d2","content":"<p>Several different species of insects use shimmering to defend against hornets.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a6a2bdf8-c464-409e-b5ec-a73aa3c4cbdc"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The text describes a study about how giant honeybees use shimmering to defend against hornets, but not other predators. The researchers conclude that shimmering could be a specialized defense response to hornets. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The text says that giant honeybees don&rsquo;t appear to use shimmering against other, larger predators. However, it never suggests that researchers don&rsquo;t know which other defenses giant honeybees do use for those predators. Choice C is incorrect. The text says that hornets are one predator of giant honeybees, but it doesn&rsquo;t say that hornets are the main predator of giant honeybees. This choice also fails to mention &ldquo;shimmering,&rdquo; which is a major focus of the text. Choice D is incorrect. The text only discusses the shimmering of giant honeybees&mdash;it doesn&rsquo;t discuss other insects&rsquo; defense responses at all. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-CID-E010$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$53b0f7c7-ece1-460f-a604-0f76510b80f4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"23a7038f","external_id":"5a912020-242d-4bc0-96dd-c00c12736562","vaultid":"4658a564-1792-494d-9fdf-a8f541d02efd","uId":"bb790814-9eeb-462d-9d08-c9fa0f2742c6","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Shimmering is a collective defense behavior that researchers have observed in giant honeybee colonies. When shimmering, different groups of bees flip their bodies up and down in what looks like waves. This defense is initiated when hornets hover near a colony, serving to deter the hornets from approaching the bees. Researchers hypothesize that this behavior is a specialized defense response to hornets, as it is not observed when other, larger predators approach the colony.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"7238571c-4a6b-4cdd-b2d2-7af73c726aaf","content":"<p>Researchers are unsure how giant honeybees defend against predators larger than hornets.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"a6a2bdf8-c464-409e-b5ec-a73aa3c4cbdc","content":"<p>Researchers think that shimmering in giant honeybees is a specific defense against hornets.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"09ebed01-efe3-4221-ad1b-d60645f5cf85","content":"<p>Hornets are known to be the main predator of giant honeybees.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"cac1bb64-8101-4f4a-8346-6beae5ae78d2","content":"<p>Several different species of insects use shimmering to defend against hornets.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["a6a2bdf8-c464-409e-b5ec-a73aa3c4cbdc"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The text describes a study about how giant honeybees use shimmering to defend against hornets, but not other predators. The researchers conclude that shimmering could be a specialized defense response to hornets. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The text says that giant honeybees don&rsquo;t appear to use shimmering against other, larger predators. However, it never suggests that researchers don&rsquo;t know which other defenses giant honeybees do use for those predators. Choice C is incorrect. The text says that hornets are one predator of giant honeybees, but it doesn&rsquo;t say that hornets are the main predator of giant honeybees. This choice also fails to mention &ldquo;shimmering,&rdquo; which is a major focus of the text. Choice D is incorrect. The text only discusses the shimmering of giant honeybees&mdash;it doesn&rsquo;t discuss other insects&rsquo; defense responses at all. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-CID-E010","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"53b0f7c7-ece1-460f-a604-0f76510b80f4","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959360,"updateDate":1691007959360}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959360, 1691007959360),
    ($SATQ$23b5cb59$SATQ$, $SATQ$f74ea265-3bf1-46e7-bf80-ba2fc5b139ed$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><figure class="table"><table class="gdr"><caption style="caption-side: top;"><p style="text-align: center;">Total Electoral College Votes and Popular Votes in the 15th US Presidential Election</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Candidate</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Total electoral college votes</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Total popular votes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">James K. Polk</th><td style="text-align: center;">170</td><td style="text-align: center;">1,339,494</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Henry Clay</th><td style="text-align: center;">105</td><td style="text-align: center;">1,300,004</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">James Gillespie Birney</th><td style="text-align: center;">0</td><td style="text-align: center;">62,103</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>
<p>A student is writing a paper on the history of the 15th presidential election in the United States, which was held in 1844. The student needs to determine how many electoral college votes were cast for the winning candidate, James K. Polk. According to the table, James K. Polk received <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"fa2cbf70-5a3f-47ae-9b70-13d8ae4af2a5","content":"<p>170 electoral college votes.</p>"},{"id":"3d8ed871-59e5-410a-88bb-cb0164778354","content":"<p>103 electoral college votes.</p>"},{"id":"0e86dc22-66a6-4a23-8d2d-5657776d33cd","content":"<p>62 electoral college votes.</p>"},{"id":"1c3d4a9b-945e-42ae-a004-78746bd861ea","content":"<p>44 electoral college votes.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["fa2cbf70-5a3f-47ae-9b70-13d8ae4af2a5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement about the 15th US presidential election. The table shows the total number of electoral college votes and the total number of popular votes received by each of three candidates. The table shows that Polk received 170 electoral college votes.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the table shows that Polk received 170 electoral college votes, not 103. According to the table, none of the candidates received a total of 103 votes of either type (electoral college or popular). Choice C is incorrect because the table shows that Polk received 170 electoral college votes, not 62. According to the table, none of the candidates received a total of 62 votes of either type (electoral college or popular). Choice D is incorrect because the table shows that Polk received 170 electoral college votes, not 44. According to the table, none of the candidates received a total of 44 votes of either type (electoral college or popular).</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"23b5cb59","external_id":"f74ea265-3bf1-46e7-bf80-ba2fc5b139ed","vaultid":null,"uId":"9a8ae971-5a0d-48ec-8236-77219bb4b4bc","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><figure class=\"table\"><table class=\"gdr\"><caption style=\"caption-side: top;\"><p style=\"text-align: center;\">Total Electoral College Votes and Popular Votes in the 15th US Presidential Election</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Candidate</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Total electoral college votes</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Total popular votes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">James K. Polk</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">170</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">1,339,494</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Henry Clay</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">105</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">1,300,004</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">James Gillespie Birney</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">0</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">62,103</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>\n<p>A student is writing a paper on the history of the 15th presidential election in the United States, which was held in 1844. The student needs to determine how many electoral college votes were cast for the winning candidate, James K. Polk. According to the table, James K. Polk received <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?</p>","answer